# Formatting

`howdy/i18n/format` writes numbers, money, dates, relative times and lists
the way a translator's locale writes them. The patterns, names and plural
rules come from the Unicode [CLDR](https://cldr.unicode.org/), the data
browsers use for `Intl`.

```gleam
import howdy/i18n
import howdy/i18n/format

let t = i18n.new("de")
```

## Numbers

```gleam
format.integer(t, 1_234_567)   // "1.234.567"
format.decimal(t, 3.14159)     // "3,142", up to three decimal places
format.fixed(t, 2.5, 2)        // "2,50", exactly two
format.percent(t, 0.25)        // "25 %"
```

Each locale has its own grouping. Indian English writes `12,34,567`, and
Spanish leaves four-digit numbers ungrouped. Persian writes its own digits,
as `۱٬۲۳۴`.

## Money

Amounts are given in the currency's smallest unit, such as cents, so no
amount is ever a float:

```gleam
format.currency(t, minor: 123_456, code: "EUR")             // "1.234,56 €"
format.currency(i18n.new("en"), minor: 1999, code: "USD")   // "$19.99"
format.currency(i18n.new("en"), minor: 1234, code: "JPY")   // "¥1,234"
```

Each currency has its own number of decimal places: yen have none, and the
Kuwaiti dinar has three. A currency without a symbol in the locale's data
shows its code.

## Dates and times

Dates and times use `gleam/time/calendar`:

```gleam
let date = calendar.Date(2026, calendar.October, 19)
let time = calendar.TimeOfDay(15, 4, 0, 0)

format.date(t, date, format.Short)       // "19.10.26"
format.date(t, date, format.Long)        // "19. Oktober 2026"
format.date(t, date, format.Full)        // "Montag, 19. Oktober 2026"
format.time(t, time, format.Short)       // "15:04"
format.date_time(t, date, time, format.Medium)
format.month_year(t, 2026, calendar.October)   // "Oktober 2026"
```

There is no time zone support yet, so convert to the reader's zone before
formatting. The zone names some locales put in their longer time formats
are left out.

For a calendar of your own, `format.months`, `format.weekdays` (Monday
first) and `format.first_day_of_week` give the names and the day weeks
start on where the locale is used.

## Relative times

```gleam
format.relative(i18n.new("en"), -1, format.Day)    // "yesterday"
format.relative(i18n.new("en"), -3, format.Day)    // "3 days ago"
format.relative(i18n.new("en"), 2, format.Hour)    // "in 2 hours"
```

Where the locale has a word for the offset, it is used, such as
"yesterday", "today" and "tomorrow".

## Lists

```gleam
format.list(i18n.new("en"), ["Ann", "Ben", "Cem"])      // "Ann, Ben, and Cem"
format.or_list(i18n.new("en"), ["tea", "coffee"])       // "tea or coffee"
format.list(i18n.new("de"), ["Ann", "Ben", "Cem"])      // "Ann, Ben und Cem"
```

## Which locales have data

Formatting data is included for 46 locales:

- Arabic, Bengali, Bulgarian, Chinese (simplified and traditional),
  Croatian, Czech, Danish and Dutch
- English, for the US, UK, Australia, Canada and India
- Finnish, French (and for Canada and Switzerland), German (and for Austria
  and Switzerland), Greek and Hebrew
- Hindi, Hungarian, Indonesian, Italian, Japanese, Korean, Malay and
  Norwegian
- Persian, Polish, Portuguese (Brazil and Portugal) and Romanian
- Russian, Slovak, Spanish (and for Latin America and Mexico), Swedish,
  Thai, Turkish, Ukrainian and Vietnamese

`i18n.data_locales()` lists them. Any other locale uses the nearest one
that has data, and English in the end. Plural rules cover every language
CLDR knows.
